WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.401 --> 00:00:03.369
-Our first guest tonight
is an Emmy-nominated writer

00:00:03.369 --> 00:00:05.237
and comedian you know
from "Saturday Night Live,"

00:00:05.237 --> 00:00:06.573
where he serves
as co-head writer

00:00:06.573 --> 00:00:08.008
and co-anchor of
"Weekend Update."

00:00:08.008 --> 00:00:11.176
Here's my conversation
with Michael Che.

00:00:11.176 --> 00:00:15.047
Michael, I cannot tell you
how happy I am to see you.

00:00:15.047 --> 00:00:18.551
You're in Manhattan right now.
How is it?

00:00:18.551 --> 00:00:20.720
-It's wild, man.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:25.024
I'm so tired, too, by the way,
because I don't --

00:00:25.024 --> 00:00:26.825
I didn't even sleep at all.

00:00:26.825 --> 00:00:29.295
-You grew up in New York.

00:00:29.295 --> 00:00:31.398
And have you ever --

00:00:31.398 --> 00:00:36.136
Have you ever felt this way
about the city you call home?

00:00:36.136 --> 00:00:40.640
-No. You know what's so crazy?
Is that, like --

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:45.378
I remember I went to Egypt
like a long time ago.

00:00:45.378 --> 00:00:48.481
It was like 2012 or so.

00:00:48.481 --> 00:00:49.815
And I was so excited

00:00:49.815 --> 00:00:52.117
because I had never left
the country before.

00:00:52.117 --> 00:00:54.353
And it was like, "They want you
to do some shows.

00:00:54.353 --> 00:00:56.022
Would you do some shows
in Egypt?"

00:00:56.022 --> 00:00:58.825
And I was like, "Absolutely.
I would love to go to Egypt."

00:00:58.825 --> 00:01:01.460
And long story short,
when I got to Egypt,

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:02.661
I was so excited to be there,

00:01:02.661 --> 00:01:04.564
and everybody was so happy
that I was there,

00:01:04.564 --> 00:01:06.365
and they were like,
"We didn't think you'd come.

00:01:06.365 --> 00:01:08.601
Everybody said no."

00:01:08.601 --> 00:01:10.503
And I was like, "What do
you mean everybody said no?"

00:01:10.503 --> 00:01:12.838
And they're like, "Well,
because we just had a revolution

00:01:12.838 --> 00:01:14.908
and nobody wanted to fly here."

00:01:14.908 --> 00:01:17.677
And I had no idea
that they just had a revolution.

00:01:17.677 --> 00:01:20.046
So I flew over to Egypt
during a revolution,

00:01:20.046 --> 00:01:22.248
not knowing
there was a revolution.

00:01:22.248 --> 00:01:26.619
And learning
about that revolution,

00:01:26.619 --> 00:01:30.089
it's eerily similar
seeing what's happening now

00:01:30.089 --> 00:01:32.158
to what
they were describing then,

00:01:32.158 --> 00:01:36.796
which is kind of scary
and crazy surreal.

00:01:36.796 --> 00:01:41.633
-Your last stand-up special,
you talked a lot about race.

00:01:41.633 --> 00:01:43.736
You talked a lot about

00:01:43.736 --> 00:01:46.372
the black community's
relationship with police.

00:01:46.372 --> 00:01:48.941
And this then seems to be a clip

00:01:48.941 --> 00:01:52.644
that resurfaces every time
something like this happens.

00:01:52.644 --> 00:01:54.180
-Yeah. It's kind of --

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:57.349
It's almost bittersweet
because I'm happy

00:01:57.349 --> 00:01:58.751
that people like the clip,

00:01:58.751 --> 00:02:03.122
but it's also kind of a bummer
that it's still relevant.

00:02:03.122 --> 00:02:05.157
I wish it wasn't, you know?

00:02:05.157 --> 00:02:06.759
It's almost like --

00:02:06.759 --> 00:02:08.927
I feel like the guy
who wrote "Amazing Grace."

00:02:08.927 --> 00:02:10.763
Like, every time --
You're happy you hear the song,

00:02:10.763 --> 00:02:11.764
but every time you hear it,

00:02:11.764 --> 00:02:13.332
you're like,
"Oh, no, what happened?"

00:02:13.332 --> 00:02:17.336
Because no one plays it
for a good occasion.

00:02:17.336 --> 00:02:19.939
-I wonder -- Yeah, I never
thought of it that way.

00:02:19.939 --> 00:02:21.674
I wonder when he wrote it
if he thought,

00:02:21.674 --> 00:02:24.010
"Well, this will be -- We'll
hear this a couple of times."

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:25.211
-Yeah.

00:02:25.211 --> 00:02:27.280
[ Laughter ]

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:28.881
Oh, and it's never
for a good thing.

00:02:28.881 --> 00:02:30.983
No one's ever like,
"You know what I want to hear?

00:02:30.983 --> 00:02:32.919
'Amazing Grace.'"

00:02:32.919 --> 00:02:37.022
-Will you just tell us sort of
the seed of the stand-up bit

00:02:37.022 --> 00:02:39.391
that sort of keeps coming back?

00:02:39.391 --> 00:02:42.996
-It was this black --
I don't remember. It's a bit.

00:02:42.996 --> 00:02:45.632
It's a Black Lives Matter bit
of just about --

00:02:45.632 --> 00:02:48.067
I mean, the sentiment being --
I think what people kind of

00:02:48.067 --> 00:02:52.138
connect with is that
it's just saying

00:02:52.138 --> 00:02:54.540
that we're just asking
to matter,

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:57.176
and somehow
that becomes a challenge

00:02:57.176 --> 00:03:01.413
or somehow that becomes
unreasonable to some people.

00:03:01.413 --> 00:03:08.387
And the absurdity of not being
able to give us that much,

00:03:08.387 --> 00:03:10.656
you know, is really all it is.

00:03:10.656 --> 00:03:14.560
-And, I mean, that's, of course,
speaking to people

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:17.130
who really like to stress
whenever they hear

00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:19.699
"Black Lives Matter"
that all lives matter.

00:03:19.699 --> 00:03:23.903
-Yeah.
-It must be frustrating.

00:03:23.903 --> 00:03:27.240
And it must be frustrating
that people stand in the way,

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:31.010
it seems like, of the idea
of even equal rights.

00:03:33.746 --> 00:03:37.550
-It's almost like what --
[Groans] I don't know.

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:42.689
It's kind of hard to --
After a while, you stop --

00:03:42.689 --> 00:03:45.325
Um...

00:03:47.493 --> 00:03:50.930
you stop worrying about why they
don't want to give it to you

00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:55.868
and you get to the point
where you just demand it.

00:03:55.868 --> 00:03:58.871
And I think that's
what we're seeing now.

00:03:58.871 --> 00:04:03.943
We've been asking for so long
that people are at a point

00:04:03.943 --> 00:04:05.978
where they don't want
to ask anymore,

00:04:05.978 --> 00:04:09.682
and that's kind of the reality,
and it's sad.

00:04:09.682 --> 00:04:14.487
-This must be an
incredibly hard time for you

00:04:14.487 --> 00:04:17.856
for a number of reasons,
but you have a brother

00:04:17.856 --> 00:04:20.493
who's a detective in the New
York City Police Department.

00:04:20.493 --> 00:04:23.329
Do you talk to him
through times like this?

00:04:23.329 --> 00:04:28.500
-No, I don't really like to talk
to him about stuff like this.

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:33.439
[ Laughs ] Well, I'll make
fun of him in hindsight.

00:04:33.439 --> 00:04:36.175
But, you know,
I can only imagine

00:04:36.175 --> 00:04:43.549
what it must be like for him
to grow up the way we grew up

00:04:43.549 --> 00:04:49.288
and also have to do his job
with people who police

00:04:49.288 --> 00:04:53.759
the way, you know, some of these
guys police, so...

00:04:53.759 --> 00:04:58.164
-You have another --
Am I correct?

00:04:58.164 --> 00:05:01.267
You have another brother
who's a retired police...

00:05:01.267 --> 00:05:04.070
-I have two brothers
who are cops.

00:05:04.070 --> 00:05:06.338
I have four brothers
and two sisters.

00:05:06.338 --> 00:05:09.741
Two of them are NYPD.

00:05:09.741 --> 00:05:13.246
Listen. My mother wasn't
raising rocket scientists.

00:05:13.246 --> 00:05:16.448
We're cops and comedians
and barbers.

00:05:16.448 --> 00:05:20.386
[ Laughs ]

00:05:20.386 --> 00:05:23.722
But, yeah, you know, I mean,
they get it, you know?

00:05:23.722 --> 00:05:25.958
Like, they're from where
we're from, but, you know,

00:05:25.958 --> 00:05:29.795
it's still -- I don't know.
It's a weird thing.

00:05:29.795 --> 00:05:32.898
I bet -- I bet --

00:05:32.898 --> 00:05:37.603
I don't think I've ever
dialed 911 in my life.

00:05:39.605 --> 00:05:43.775
-Just because you don't think
that they'll be helpful

00:05:43.775 --> 00:05:48.381
or that you can't imagine
that the outcome will improve?

00:05:48.381 --> 00:05:54.620
-Because I have a feeling
that they're not for me,

00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:58.557
and it's a really sad,
unfortunate thing

00:05:58.557 --> 00:06:00.059
that you don't realize

00:06:00.059 --> 00:06:02.995
until you come across people
who don't feel that way.

00:06:02.995 --> 00:06:06.465
Does that make sense?
-It certainly does.

00:06:06.465 --> 00:06:11.070
You know, listening to people

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:15.975
explain why they're
in the street protesting,

00:06:15.975 --> 00:06:18.143
it does seem like,
like you said,

00:06:18.143 --> 00:06:21.113
it's a breaking point,
it's a turning point.

00:06:21.113 --> 00:06:22.915
Do you feel -- Do you have

00:06:22.915 --> 00:06:27.719
any optimism that anything
will come of this?

00:06:27.719 --> 00:06:30.689
-I think the optimism

00:06:30.689 --> 00:06:36.128
can be seen in these
young people especially,

00:06:36.128 --> 00:06:40.199
the ability to unite
and be on the same accord.

00:06:40.199 --> 00:06:45.904
And it's coming across
as extremely destructive,

00:06:45.904 --> 00:06:49.175
but I do think
if they channeled it

00:06:49.175 --> 00:06:51.577
that they can get a lot done

00:06:51.577 --> 00:06:54.747
that generations before them
couldn't get done.

00:06:54.747 --> 00:06:58.851
And we're seeing their power.

00:06:58.851 --> 00:07:02.487
I think that's optimistic.

00:07:02.487 --> 00:07:06.124
But also, you know, now it's --

00:07:06.124 --> 00:07:08.527
You know, a lot of times
people come on these shows

00:07:08.527 --> 00:07:11.130
and they will speak
for the community.

00:07:11.130 --> 00:07:14.367
And this is
one of those rare times

00:07:14.367 --> 00:07:16.469
where you don't have to
speak for the community

00:07:16.469 --> 00:07:19.372
because the community is
actually speaking for themselves

00:07:19.372 --> 00:07:21.507
louder than they ever have.

00:07:21.507 --> 00:07:25.111
And so it's really up to them.

00:07:25.111 --> 00:07:27.246
It's really up to us
as a community,

00:07:27.246 --> 00:07:31.050
but it's really up
to this younger generation

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:36.021
who's tearing stuff apart,
you know?

00:07:36.021 --> 00:07:39.992
And, uh...

00:07:39.992 --> 00:07:42.895
I hope something good
comes of it.

00:07:42.895 --> 00:07:47.966
-I want to talk to you more
about what it's like for a

00:07:47.966 --> 00:07:51.737
comedian like you to be living
through self-distancing.

00:07:51.737 --> 00:07:54.740
We'll be right back with more
Michael Che right after this.

